\title{Preferred Semantics and Downward-Closed Topology\\in Finite Argumentation Frameworks}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 19, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We examine a proposed topological interpretation of finite abstract argumentation. Preferred extensions---maximal admissible collections of mutually compatible, self-defending arguments---are natural candidates for faces of a simplicial complex. This identification is generally false: preferred extensions form an antichain under inclusion and need not be downward closed. We give a canonical correction by taking their downward closure. The resulting preferred-generated complex has the preferred extensions as its maximal faces and therefore preserves the maximal semantic data while admitting simplicial invariants. A two-argument mutual-attack framework provides the minimal obstruction to treating the preferred family itself as a complex. A two-argument attack-free framework refutes a proposed identity relating argument and attack counts, higher homology, the number of preferred extensions, and grounded-extension size: its two sides are $2$ and $-1$. We explain why homology of the corrected complex measures overlap patterns among coherent positions rather than directed attack cycles themselves, present exhaustive algorithms, and formulate directions based on intersection posets, collapses, cycle detection, and realizability.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
A finite argumentation framework records arguments and directed attacks. Its semantics asks which collections can be jointly accepted. Preferred extensions represent inclusion-maximal positions that are internally conflict-free and defend all their members. Their plurality suggests a geometric question: can coherent positions be studied through simplicial topology?

The proposal is attractive because simplicial complexes encode compatibility of arbitrary order. Homology distinguishes connected components, unfilled loops, and higher-dimensional cavities. The obstacle is structural: preferred extensions are maximal objects, whereas a simplicial complex contains every face together with all its subsets.

We prove that preferred extensions form an antichain. A two-argument mutual-attack framework then shows that they are not generally downward closed. The canonical repair is to include every subset of every preferred extension. This preferred-generated complex preserves the preferred extensions exactly as its facets.

This correction changes the interpretation of topology. An attack is directed opposition; a simplicial edge means that two arguments coexist in some preferred extension. Consequently, a directed attack cycle need not produce first homology. We also refute the proposed universal equation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:proposed}
 |A|-|R|+\sum_{n\ge2}(-1)^n\dim H_n
 =\#\{\text{preferred extensions}\}-|G|,
\end{equation}
where $G$ is grounded. On two isolated arguments, the two sides are $2$ and $-1$.

\section{Argumentation semantics}

\begin{definition}[Finite argumentation framework]
A finite argumentation framework is a pair $F=(A,R)$, where $A$ is a finite set and $R\subseteq A\times A$. If $(a,b)\in R$, then $a$ attacks $b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Conflict freedom]
A subset $S\subseteq A$ is conflict-free if there are no $a,b\in S$ with $(a,b)\in R$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Defence]
A subset $S\subseteq A$ defends $a\in A$ if, for every $b\in A$ with $(b,a)\in R$, there exists $c\in S$ such that $(c,b)\in R$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Admissibility and preferred extension]
A subset $S\subseteq A$ is admissible if it is conflict-free and defends every member of $S$. A preferred extension is an inclusion-maximal admissible subset.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Complete and grounded extensions]
A subset $C\subseteq A$ is complete if it is conflict-free and, for every $a\in A$, one has $a\in C$ exactly when $C$ defends $a$. A grounded extension is an inclusion-least complete extension.
\end{definition}

The empty set is always admissible: conflict freedom and defence of its members are both vacuous. Preferred semantics seeks maximal defensible positions; grounded semantics identifies the cautious least complete position.

\begin{theorem}[Preferred Antichain Theorem]\label{thm:antichain}
If $P$ and $Q$ are preferred extensions and $P\subseteq Q$, then $P=Q$. Thus distinct preferred extensions are incomparable under inclusion.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The set $Q$ is admissible. Since $P$ is maximal admissible and $P\subseteq Q$, maximality gives $Q\subseteq P$. Antisymmetry yields equality.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Nonempty admissibility excludes the empty preferred set]\label{lem:empty}
If a framework has a nonempty admissible set $S$, then $\varnothing$ is not preferred.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $\varnothing$ were preferred, maximality applied to the admissible superset $S$ would give $S\subseteq\varnothing$, contradicting $S\ne\varnothing$.
\end{proof}

\section{The preferred-generated complex}

\begin{definition}[Abstract simplicial complex]
An abstract simplicial complex on $A$ is a family $K\subseteq2^A$ such that $S\in K$ and $T\subseteq S$ imply $T\in K$. Its members are faces and its inclusion-maximal faces are facets. A face with $k+1$ vertices has dimension $k$.
\end{definition}

The antichain theorem indicates that preferred extensions are candidates for facets, not for the entire face family.

\begin{definition}[Preferred-generated complex]
For $F=(A,R)$, define
\[
 K_F=\{S\subseteq A:\text{there is a preferred extension }P\text{ with }S\subseteq P\}.
\]
This is the downward closure of the preferred extensions.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Generated-Complex Theorem]\label{thm:complex}
The family $K_F$ is an abstract simplicial complex. Every preferred extension is a face, and the facets of $K_F$ are exactly the preferred extensions.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $S\subseteq P$ for a preferred extension $P$ and $T\subseteq S$, then $T\subseteq P$, proving downward closure. Each preferred extension $P$ belongs because $P\subseteq P$. If $S$ is a facet, choose preferred $P$ with $S\subseteq P$; since $P$ is a face, maximality gives $S=P$. Conversely, a preferred extension cannot be properly contained in a face, since that face lies in another preferred extension, contradicting Theorem~\ref{thm:antichain}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Minimality]
The complex $K_F$ is the smallest abstract simplicial complex containing every preferred extension.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Any complex containing $P$ contains every subset of $P$. It therefore contains $K_F$, while Theorem~\ref{thm:complex} shows that $K_F$ itself has the required property.
\end{proof}

Fix a field $\kk$. Let $C_n(K_F;\kk)$ be generated by the $n$-faces, with boundary maps $\partial_n$. The homology is
\[
 H_n(K_F;\kk)=\ker\partial_n/\operatorname{im}\partial_{n+1},
\]
and $\beta_n=\dim H_n$ is the $n$th Betti number. Here $\beta_0$ counts components, while $\beta_1$ and $\beta_2$ count independent unfilled cycles and cavities. For a finite complex,
\[
 \chi(K_F)=\sum_{n\ge0}(-1)^nf_n=\sum_{n\ge0}(-1)^n\beta_n,
\]
where $f_n$ counts $n$-faces.

A simplicial edge $\{a,b\}$ means that $a$ and $b$ coexist in a preferred extension. It does not assert an attack in either direction. Thus $|R|$ is not generally $f_1$, and attack cycles are not automatically homology classes.

\section{The mutual-attack obstruction}
Let $A=\{0,1\}$, with attacks $0R1$ and $1R0$ and no self-attacks.

\begin{lemma}[Singleton admissibility]
Each singleton $\{a\}$ is admissible.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It is conflict-free because there is no self-attack. Its only attacker is the other argument, which $a$ attacks in return.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Preferred extensions under mutual attack]\label{thm:mutual}
In the two-argument mutual-attack framework, $S$ is preferred if and only if $|S|=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each singleton is admissible and cannot be enlarged to $A$, which is not conflict-free; hence it is preferred. Conversely, a preferred set cannot be empty by Lemma~\ref{lem:empty}, and cannot contain both arguments because they attack each other. It therefore has one element.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Failure of downward closure]
Preferred extensions do not generally form a simplicial complex.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The set $\{0\}$ is preferred, but its subset $\varnothing$ is not.
\end{proof}

Here
\[
 K_F=\{\varnothing,\{0\},\{1\}\}.
\]
It consists of two isolated vertices, so $\beta_0=2$, $\beta_n=0$ for $n\ge1$, and $\chi=2$. The attack graph nevertheless contains a directed $2$-cycle. This is the minimal separation between attack cycles and topological holes.

\section{The attack-free boundary case}
Let $F=(A,\varnothing)$.

\begin{lemma}[Universal admissibility without attacks]
Every subset $S\subseteq A$ is admissible.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
No internal attacks exist, and every defence condition is vacuous.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique preferred extension without attacks]\label{thm:noattackpref}
In a finite attack-free framework, the unique preferred extension is $A$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The set $A$ is admissible. Every proper subset lies in the larger admissible set $A$ and is not maximal.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Grounded extension without attacks]\label{thm:noattackground}
In a finite attack-free framework, the unique complete extension, and hence the grounded extension, is $A$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every set defends every argument vacuously. A complete extension contains exactly what it defends, so it must equal $A$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Simplex structure]
For an attack-free framework, $K_F=2^A$. If $A$ is nonempty, then $K_F$ is a full simplex, $\beta_0=1$, $\beta_n=0$ for $n\ge1$, and $\chi(K_F)=1$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The unique preferred extension is $A$, whose downward closure is $2^A$. A full simplex is contractible.
\end{proof}

\section{Failure of the proposed identity}
Let $p(F)$ denote the number of preferred extensions and let $G$ be grounded.

\begin{theorem}[Two-isolated-argument obstruction]
Equation~\eqref{eq:proposed} is false, even for an attack-free framework on two arguments.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $|A|=2$ and $R=\varnothing$. Theorems~\ref{thm:noattackpref} and~\ref{thm:noattackground} give $p(F)=1$ and $|G|=2$. The complex is a simplex, so $\beta_n=0$ for $n\ge1$. The left side of~\eqref{eq:proposed} is
\[
 2-0+0=2,
\]
whereas the right side is
\[
 1-2=-1.
\]
Thus the proposed equality would require $2=-1$.
\end{proof}

The example also diagnoses the misuse of ``Euler characteristic'' in the proposed left side. The complex is a filled edge with $f_0=2$ and $f_1=1$, so its genuine Euler characteristic is $2-1=1$. The attack count $|R|=0$ is not the edge count. Even replacing the left side by the true Euler characteristic would leave $1\ne-1$.

Euler characteristic depends on the whole face structure, hence on intersections among preferred extensions. The scalar quantities $|A|$, $|R|$, $p(F)$, and $|G|$ do not retain these intersections.

\section{Algorithms}
\subsection{Preferred-extension enumeration}
For $n=|A|$, represent subsets by $n$-bit masks. Enumerate all $2^n$ candidates. For each candidate, check every ordered internal pair for conflict freedom; then, for each member and each attacker, search within the candidate for a counterattacker. Retain admissible candidates and discard each one properly contained in another retained candidate. A direct implementation takes $O(2^n n^3)$ time and output-sensitive exponential storage. Precomputed attacker masks improve constants.

\subsection{Generated-complex construction}
Given preferred extensions $P_1,\dots,P_m$, enumerate every subset of every $P_i$ and insert it into a deduplicating set. The time is
\[
 O\!\left(\sum_{i=1}^m2^{|P_i|}\right)
\]
up to hashing, with $O(|K_F|)$ storage. The preferred extensions are already the facets by Theorem~\ref{thm:complex}.

\subsection{Homology over $\Ftwo$}
Sort faces by dimension. Form $B_k$ with columns indexed by $k$-faces and rows by $(k-1)$-faces; an entry is $1$ exactly when the row face is obtained by deleting one vertex from the column face. Gaussian elimination gives ranks $r_k$, and
\[
 \beta_k=f_k-r_k-r_{k+1}.
\]
Dense elimination is cubic in matrix dimensions, while sparse column reduction exploits the $k+1$ nonzero boundary entries of each $k$-simplex.

\subsection{Grounded semantics}
For small frameworks, enumerate conflict-free sets, retain those containing exactly the arguments they defend, and select the inclusion-least one. This is exponential in the worst case but transparent for numerical experiments; fixed-point methods are preferable at larger scales.

\section{Applications and interpretation}
The preferred-generated complex is a higher-order compatibility object. Vertices are arguments occurring in preferred extensions; edges are jointly occurring pairs; triangles are jointly occurring triples. Components can represent clusters linked by chains of semantic compatibility. A first-homology class is a cyclic pattern of coexistence not filled by larger coexistence; a second-homology class is a shell of compatible triples without a compatible quadruple filling it.

These meanings concern semantic compatibility, not rhetorical quality. To analyze transcripts, one must first identify arguments and directed attacks. The resulting finite framework can then be processed deterministically: enumerate preferred extensions, take their downward closure, and compute face numbers and homology. A topological loop may reflect overlapping defensible camps; it does not by itself certify circular reasoning in ordinary discourse.

The construction also permits sensitivity analysis. Adding an attack can remove admissible sets, alter facets, disconnect the complex, or eliminate a high-dimensional face. A new argument lying in every preferred extension may create a cone and destroy positive-dimensional homology.

\section{Discussion and future work}
Maximal semantics and simplicial closure must be separated. Preferred extensions are facets by nature. Their canonical downward closure adds all partial positions without changing the maximal positions.

The topology belongs to semantic coexistence rather than to attacks directly. Defence can suppress or enable combinations in ways not captured by directed cycles. Any cycle-to-homology theorem therefore requires defence-sensitive hypotheses.

A useful corrected counting perspective writes
\[
 K_F=\bigcup_i\Delta(P_i),
\]
where $\Delta(P_i)$ is the simplex on preferred extension $P_i$. Every nonempty intersection is the simplex on $P_{i_1}\cap\cdots\cap P_{i_k}$. The intersection poset can therefore determine Euler characteristic through inclusion--exclusion, whereas the number of preferred extensions alone cannot.

Future work includes testing whether strong preferred-semantic equivalence controls simple-homotopy type after unused vertices are removed; characterizing first homology for directed cactus graphs by parity and defence; finding grounded-core collapse criteria from unique defenders; proving limits on affine formulas from coarse scalar counts; and determining which finite simplicial complexes are realizable, up to homotopy or isomorphism, by preferred-generated complexes.

\section{Conclusion}
Preferred extensions do not generally form a simplicial complex; they form an antichain of maximal admissible positions. Their downward closure is the canonical complex, with preferred extensions exactly as facets. Two mutual attackers demonstrate failure of raw downward closure, while two isolated arguments refute the proposed Euler--semantics formula by forcing $2\ne-1$.

The holes of a debate are consequently holes in the overlap geometry of maximal defensible positions, not automatically cycles in its directed attack graph. This distinction provides a mathematically coherent foundation for the topology of argumentation.

