# Explore the Shape of a Debate

A debate begins as a directed network of attacks, but its topology comes from a different relation: which arguments can coexist inside a maximal self-defending position. Start with the smallest example.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The two maximal positions are incomparable. That is the **antichain principle**. Click from the preferred family to its downward closure to see why the empty face must be added.

<details><summary>Reveal the definitions behind the explorer</summary>
A set is conflict-free when it contains no internal attack. It defends one of its arguments when it counterattacks every attacker of that argument. An admissible set is conflict-free and defends all its members. A preferred extension is an inclusion-maximal admissible set. The preferred-generated complex contains every subset of every preferred extension.
</details>

## Compute maximal positions

{{algorithm:0}}

Run the complete numerical study to compare mutual attack, isolation, and a directed three-cycle.

{{demo:0}}

The attack graph can contain a cycle while the semantic complex has no loop. The diagram makes that mismatch visible.

{{visualization:0}}

For background, see the [Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy overview of argumentation and logic](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-argumentation/) and the [Encyclopedia of Mathematics entry on simplicial complexes](https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Simplicial_complex).

## From maximal positions to all faces

{{algorithm:1}}

Overlaps among facets—not merely their number—control the topology. Experiment with three overlapping positions.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

<details><summary>Why intersections determine Euler characteristic</summary>
The complex is a union of full simplices, one on each preferred extension. Every nonempty intersection is itself the simplex on the intersection of those extensions. Inclusion–exclusion can therefore recover the Euler characteristic from the intersection poset.
</details>

## The smallest numerical obstruction

For two isolated arguments, the unique preferred and grounded extension is the full pair. Its generated complex is a filled edge.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

The proposed expression gives $2$, while the semantic count gives $-1$. The genuine Euler characteristic is a third number, $1$, because attacks are not simplicial edges.

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the full calculation</summary>
There are two arguments and zero attacks. Higher Betti numbers vanish, so the proposed left side is $2-0+0=2$. There is one preferred extension and the grounded extension has size two, so the right side is $1-2=-1$. The complex has two vertices and one edge, hence genuine Euler characteristic $2-1=1$.
</details>

## Measure holes

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

The central lesson is precise: topology measures the higher-order overlap of coherent positions. A directed attack cycle may contribute to that geometry, but defence and higher-dimensional compatibility decide whether a hole survives. Learn more from the [homology overview](https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Homology) and use the widgets above to build intuition before opening the detailed arguments.

